'' posix.ti -- the POSIX constants and struct layouts of this target, measured: the binding
'' the std modules' __FB_UNIX__ code is written against.
''
''     #ifdef __FB_UNIX__
''     #include once "../../sys/posix/posix.ti"
''     using posix
''     #endif
''
''     dim st as struct_stat
''     if( lstat( p, @st ) = 0 ) andalso ( ( st.st_mode and S_IFMT ) = S_IFDIR ) then ...
''
'' INTERNAL. It exists so pkg/std carries no OS value of its own (docs/tiko-macos RFC-0001 §4,
'' decision D2): every O_, AT_, errno, signal, termios, socket, locale and fenv constant, and
'' every struct the std modules hand to libc (struct_stat, struct_termios, struct_sockaddr_in,
'' ...), comes from <target>-posix.ti beside this file. build/posixgen.sh writes that file on
'' the target's own host, from build/posix.spec, by compiling and running a C probe -- the
'' values are what the C compiler says, never typed in -- and asserts every struct size and
'' offset, so a layout tiko would lay out differently fails to compile rather than read the
'' wrong bytes. tiko-rt includes the same bytes as lib/rt/src/inc/sysbind/<target>-posix.ti;
'' build/lint.sh holds the two copies identical.
''
'' A HEADER, NOT A MODULE, AND ONE NAMESPACE. The plan was a facade module, '#import
'' "sys/posix"'. Measured with tiko and seed-6: a module's public names reach every program
'' that imports the module that imported it -- privately, with or without 'using' -- far
'' enough that the program can no longer include crt.bi, whose headers #define O_RDONLY,
'' EPERM, CLOCKS_PER_SEC and the rest (TKO0371); tiko's own units include crt.bi and import
'' std/fs. A private include goes no further than the module's own names, EXCEPT that a std
'' module's interface replays its libc declares, which name these types, and so carries this
'' header to every importer too. Hence the namespace: the binding is posix.O_RDONLY to
'' everyone, which no macro of crt.bi collides with, and the including module says
'' 'using posix' to write it bare.
''
'' NAMES ARE C's. A constant keeps its macro's name (O_CREAT, EINTR, TIOCGWINSZ); a struct is
'' struct_<tag> (struct_stat), so it can never collide with the C function of the same name
'' -- stat(), sigaction(), flock() -- in tiko's case-insensitive namespace; a C typedef keeps
'' its name (pthread_mutex_t, sigset_t, socklen_t). Functions are not here: each module
'' declares the libc calls it makes, with these types. Every name is in the including module's
'' scope, so a module-local may not reuse one (TKO0945), and the module may not include crt.bi.
''
'' A name missing for a target is the binding's to add (build/posix.spec), never a module's
'' to define.

#pragma once

#if defined( __FB_LINUX__ )
    #include once "linux-x86_64-posix.ti"
#elseif defined( __FB_DARWIN__ )
    #include once "macos-arm64-posix.ti"
#elseif defined( __FB_UNIX__ )
    #error "sys/posix: no binding for this target yet -- run build/posixgen.sh on its host"
#else
    #error "sys/posix is for POSIX targets: build for -target linux-x86_64 or macos-arm64"
#endif
